#: ../../../artwork.rst:3
msgid "Artwork"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:8
msgid "Setuptools logo, designed in 2021 by `Anderson Bravalheri`_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:11
msgid "Elements of Design"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:13
msgid ""
"The main colours of the design are a dark pastel azure (``#336790``) and a "
"pale orange (``#E5B62F``), referred in this document simply as \"blue\" and "
"\"yellow\" respectively. The text uses the *Monoid* typeface, an open source "
"webfont that was developed by Andreas Larsen and contributors in 2015 and is "
"distributed under the MIT or SIL licenses (more information at https://"
"github.com/larsenwork/monoid)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:22
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:24
msgid ""
"The preferred way of using the setuptools logo is over a white (or light) "
"background. Alternatively, the following options can be considered, "
"depending on the circumstances:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:28
msgid ""
"*\"negative\"* design - for dark backgrounds (e.g. website displayed in "
"\"dark mode\"): the white colour (``#FFFFFF``) of the background and the "
"\"blue\" (``#336790``) colour of the design can be swapped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:31
msgid ""
"*\"monochrome\"* - when colours are not available (e.g. black and white "
"printed media): a completely black or white version of the logo can also be "
"used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:33
msgid ""
"*\"banner\"* mode: the symbol and text can be used alongside depending on "
"the available space."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:36
msgid "The following image illustrate these alternatives:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:41
msgid ""
"Please refer to the SVG files in the `setuptools repository`_ for the "
"specific shapes and proportions between the elements of the design."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:46
msgid "Working with the Design"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:48
msgid ""
"The `setuptools repository`_ contains a series of vector representations of "
"the design under the ``docs/images`` directory. These representations can be "
"manipulated via any graphic editor that support SVG files, however the free "
"and open-source software Inkscape_ is recommended for maximum compatibility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:54
msgid ""
"When selecting the right file to work with, file names including ``editable-"
"inkscape`` indicate \"more editable\" elements (e.g. editable text), while "
"the others prioritise SVG paths for maximum reproducibility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:58
msgid ""
"Also notice that you might have to `install the correct fonts`_ to be able "
"to visualise or edit some of the designs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:63
msgid "Inspiration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../artwork.rst:65
msgid ""
"This design was inspired by :user:`cajhne`'s `original proposal`_ and the "
"ancient symbol of the ouroboros_. It features a snake moving in a circular "
"trajectory not only as a reference to the Python programming language but "
"also to the :pep:`wheel package format <427>` as one of the distribution "
"formats supported by setuptools. The shape of the snake also resembles a "
"cog, which together with the hammer is a nod to the two words that compose "
"the name of the project."
msgstr ""

